1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,863
DiscoveryBrief helps pre-sales teams prepare
for better discovery meetings.

2
00:00:04,863 --> 00:00:10,114
It connects recent company research, customer
requirements, and questions you can

3
00:00:10,114 --> 00:00:12,967
actually use, with evidence you can inspect.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,990
Before a customer meeting, useful information
is scattered across company

5
00:00:18,990 --> 00:00:23,912
announcements, documents, and notes. A
convincing summary is not enough.

6
00:00:23,912 --> 00:00:29,313
You need to know what changed, what the
customer explicitly asked for, and what

7
00:00:29,313 --> 00:00:31,022
still needs confirmation.

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,720
Here is the real application. We enter OpenAI
and its official domain, choose a

9
00:00:37,720 --> 00:00:40,833
meeting time, and request an English brief.

10
00:00:40,833 --> 00:00:46,046
This demonstration uses a public company with
fictional meeting context.

11
00:00:46,046 --> 00:00:51,476
The sample RFP asks for single sign-on and
document repository integration.

12
00:00:51,476 --> 00:00:56,906
Private notes, participant names, and the RFP
are kept out of public search

13
00:00:56,906 --> 00:00:57,485
queries.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,659
Tavily searches the public web and extracts
selected pages.

15
00:01:03,659 --> 00:01:06,512
Every live model request goes through Bifrost.

16
00:01:06,512 --> 00:01:11,287
Separate Planner, Analyst, Verifier, Composer,
and Auditor steps organize the

17
00:01:11,287 --> 00:01:15,629
work. Application code checks dates,
references, and exact quotations.

18
00:01:15,629 --> 00:01:19,350
The research wait is omitted from this edited
demonstration.

19
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,197
The completed live result brings the meeting
context and research together.

20
00:01:25,197 --> 00:01:30,878
Recent developments are separate from RFP
requirements, questions, hypotheses, and

21
00:01:30,878 --> 00:01:35,382
unknowns. The visible quality label describes
available coverage.

22
00:01:35,382 --> 00:01:40,786
It is not an accuracy score. A hypothesis
remains something to confirm, rather

23
00:01:40,786 --> 00:01:43,350
than an assertion about the customer.

24
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,951
Open the evidence section to inspect a literal
source quotation and its link.

25
00:01:48,951 --> 00:01:53,710
Event dates help explain why a development
belongs in the research window.

26
00:01:53,710 --> 00:01:57,825
RFP requirements also retain quotations from
the submitted text.

27
00:01:57,825 --> 00:02:03,034
This lets a pre-sales engineer check the basis
of a statement before repeating it

28
00:02:03,034 --> 00:02:04,770
in a customer conversation.

29
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,610
The priority questions turn preparation into a
meeting agenda.

30
00:02:13,610 --> 00:02:17,976
They connect the supplied requirements and
available evidence to topics for

31
00:02:17,976 --> 00:02:22,751
clarification. The usage view records
operational details, including retrieval and

32
00:02:22,751 --> 00:02:27,350
model calls. Unknown cost or usage stays
unknown, instead of appearing as zero.

33
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,901
This separate example is a clearly labelled
synthetic replay.

34
00:02:31,901 --> 00:02:36,187
When no public development is verified, the
result is context only.

35
00:02:36,187 --> 00:02:41,047
DiscoveryBrief can still organize user-
provided context and questions, while

36
00:02:41,047 --> 00:02:43,350
making the missing evidence visible.

37
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,180
Export a one-page PDF and keep the detailed
evidence separately.

38
00:02:48,180 --> 00:02:53,079
DiscoveryBrief is a working local prototype,
with independent human quality

39
00:02:53,079 --> 00:02:57,325
evaluation still pending. Prepare with
context. Ask with purpose.

40
00:02:57,325 --> 00:02:59,350
Keep the evidence within reach.
